      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": -330
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "RNA-seq (STAR + Salmon), built from library nodes",
        "icon": "StickyNote",
        "noteText": "The default route of nf-core/rnaseq, rebuilt step by step from nf-core modules: read QC and trimming, STAR alignment, Salmon quantification, SAMtools statistics and a MultiQC report.\n\nEvery node is a library node. Double-click one to see its settings, and open its Code tab for the Nextflow code it adds. Duplicate the workflow to change anything.",
        "inputs": [],
        "outputs": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "note_inputs",
      "type": "note",
      "position": {
        "x": -360,
        "y": 380
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "Inputs",
        "icon": "StickyNote",
        "noteText": "Samplesheet: four samples from the nf-core/rnaseq test data (yeast), three paired-end and one single-end. The reads are downloaded on the fly.\n\nReference genome: the test genome (FASTA) and gene annotation (GTF). Swap in your own files or URLs to analyse other data.",
        "inputs": [],
        "outputs": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "note_genome",
      "type": "note",
      "position": {
        "x": 760,
        "y": 1120
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "Genome preparation",
        "icon": "StickyNote",
        "noteText": "As in nf-core/rnaseq: unzip the GTF, drop annotation on sequences that aren't in the genome, write the transcript sequences (gffread -w), and build the STAR and Salmon indexes.\n\nThe STAR index uses --genomeSAindexNbases 7 because the test genome is tiny (230 kb); remove it for a real genome.",
        "inputs": [],
        "outputs": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "note_quant",
      "type": "note",
      "position": {
        "x": 1520,
        "y": 760
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "Alignment and quantification",
        "icon": "StickyNote",
        "noteText": "STAR aligns the trimmed reads with the options nf-core/rnaseq uses and also writes alignments to the transcriptome. Salmon quantifies those (alignment mode, --libType A detects the strandedness).\n\nSAMtools sorts and indexes the genome alignments and collects statistics.",
        "inputs": [],
        "outputs": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "note_results",
      "type": "note",
      "position": {
        "x": 2280,
        "y": -220
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "Results",
        "icon": "StickyNote",
        "noteText": "Run it on the Docker install (the browser demo builds and exports only). Each node saves its outputs to results/<node name>: Salmon quantifications in results/salmon_quantification/<sample>/quant.sf, the report in results/multiqc/multiqc_report.html.\n\nCollect QC reports is a channel operator that mixes every report into one channel for MultiQC.",
        "inputs": [],
        "outputs": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "note_scope",
      "type": "note",
      "position": {
        "x": 1900,
        "y": 760
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "What the full pipeline adds",
        "icon": "StickyNote",
        "noteText": "nf-core/rnaseq also merges runs of the same sample, infers strandedness, removes rRNA and contaminants, offers other aligners (HISAT2, RSEM, Bowtie 2, Kallisto) and UMI handling, adds QC (RSeQC, Qualimap, dupRadar, Preseq) and builds gene-level count matrices. Use the nf-core/rnaseq Example to run all of it.",
        "inputs": [],
        "outputs": []
      }
    }
